From 4f6ac47b55e3ce6e982807928d6074ec105ab66e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christoph Hellwig <hch@lst.de>
Date: Thu, 28 Dec 2023 07:24:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] xfs: fix a use after free in xfs_defer_finish_recovery

dfp will be freed by ->recover_work and thus the tracepoint in case
of an error can lead to a use after free.

Store the defer ops in a local variable to avoid that.

Fixes: 7f2f7531e0d4 ("xfs: store an ops pointer in struct xfs_defer_pending")
Reported-by: kernel test robot <oliver.sang@intel.com>
Signed-off-by: Christoph Hellwig <hch@lst.de>
Reviewed-by: "Darrick J. Wong" <djwong@kernel.org>
Signed-off-by: Chandan Babu R <chandanbabu@kernel.org>

diff --git a/fs/xfs/libxfs/xfs_defer.c b/fs/xfs/libxfs/xfs_defer.c
index ca7f0ac04896..75c5b3a2c2cb 100644
--- a/fs/xfs/libxfs/xfs_defer.c
+++ b/fs/xfs/libxfs/xfs_defer.c
@@ -915,12 +915,14 @@ xfs_defer_finish_recovery(
 	struct xfs_defer_pending	*dfp,
 	struct list_head		*capture_list)
 {
+	const struct xfs_defer_op_type	*ops = dfp->dfp_ops;
 	int				error;

-	error = dfp->dfp_ops->recover_work(dfp, capture_list);
+	/* dfp is freed by recover_work and must not be accessed afterwards */
+	error = ops->recover_work(dfp, capture_list);
 	if (error)
 		trace_xlog_intent_recovery_failed(mp, error,
-				dfp->dfp_ops->recover_work);
+				ops->recover_work);
 	return error;
 }

-- 
2.25.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
error = dfp->dfp_ops->recover_work(dfp, capture_list);
trace_xlog_intent_recovery_failed(mp, error, dfp->dfp_ops->recover_work);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `dfp` structure is accessed after it has been freed by the `recover_work` function. Specifically, the code attempts to dereference `dfp->dfp_ops` after `dfp` is no longer valid, leading to a use-after-free condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
const struct xfs_defer_op_type *ops = dfp->dfp_ops;
error = ops->recover_work(dfp, capture_list);
trace_xlog_intent_recovery_failed(mp, error, ops->recover_work);
```
Reason:
The fix involves storing the `dfp->dfp_ops` pointer in a local variable `ops` before calling `recover_work`. This ensures that the pointer is still valid when accessed later, preventing the use-after-free condition.

